WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:01.360 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  from east to west from north to south  India has seen protests big and small  many extremely noisy Indians have come  out in large numbers angry at the law  passed last month granting citizenship  to people from persecuted religious  minorities from neighboring countries  but not if they're Muslim opposition  politicians civil society activists  young and old and many students have  been at the forefront but peaceful  demonstrations have turned violent in  states mainly controlled by the ruling  BJP party of Prime Minister Narendra  Modi police have been blamed for  numerous deaths and injuries in Auto  Pradesh the death of this young  restaurant worker walking home after a  shift caused outrage and prompted an  investigation into police violence  protests have taken various forms in  song and art of these creative minds  gather to explain how they feel about  the events of the past few weeks as  Indians not necessarily this or that  community of caste or religion I just  stood together in Kolkata opposition  politicians have made their position  clear regardless of religion they say  the reality of the National Register of  citizens the NRC and the citizenship  Amendment bill the CA B is becoming  clearer  thanks to Modi and char we had detention  camps and in those detention camps 60%  of people in those detention camps were  Bengali Hindus so this entire story  about NRC and CA being is not religion  centric at all it affects everybody  it affects Indian citizens Prime  Minister Narendra Modi is clear about  what the citizenship law is the Muslims  who were born on Indian soil or whose  ancestors are children of Mother India  they have nothing to do with the  citizenship law or the National Register  of citizens neither are the Muslims  being sent to any detention centers nor  are there any detention centers brothers  and sisters this is a white lie  and it's here at India's Supreme Court  that a final decision will be made the  Chief Justice of India will hear almost  60 petitions objecting to this the  citizenship law the government will get  its chance to explain the details of the  legislation and then we expect to hear  long and lengthy argument the debate  will center around whether the law is  discriminatory or whether it fails to  live up to the spirit of India's secular  democratic constitution until a verdicts  announced millions whose Indian identity  and their right to live here is being  questioned we'll continue to fear for  their future  Sahil Rawat al Jazeera New Delhi

